## Lifecycle and Ownership

Creation reads the table's partition keys from `svv_external_columns`, requires `values` to set each of them exactly
once (names compare without case), and runs `ADD PARTITION` without `IF NOT EXISTS`, so an existing partition is an
error rather than silently adopted. Statements list the keys in the table's partition-key order. Deletion runs
`DROP PARTITION`, which removes only catalog metadata; the S3 data stays.

## Reconciliation

Refresh reads the partition's location from `svv_external_partitions`; an update reads it again, runs
`PARTITION (...) SET LOCATION` for a changed `location`, and re-reads the catalog to verify the result; every other
change replaces the partition, which drops its catalog entry and adds it again without touching the S3 data.

| Change                           | Result                                                                                                   |
|----------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| `database`, `schema`, or `table` | replaces the partition: `DROP PARTITION` on the old table and `ADD PARTITION` on the new one             |
| `values`                         | replaces the partition: the values identify it; a key name spelled in another case also replaces it      |
| `location`                       | `ALTER TABLE ... PARTITION (...) SET LOCATION`, also when the new value differs only by a trailing slash |

**Drift.** Refresh compares the catalog location with the state. `svv_external_partitions` drops a folder's trailing
slash and truncates locations to 128 characters, so those forms of the configured location are not drift, and a change
made outside Terraform beyond the first 128 characters is not detected. Any other location change shows as an update
that sets the configured location again. A partition that is gone, or whose table, external schema, or database is gone,
or whose table no longer has the configured partition keys, is removed from state, and the next plan adds it again. An
update fails when the partition disappeared after the plan; refresh and plan again.

